Drives its own private GC state (its own `gcBindMemory`/`gcInit`-bound arena) and
every assertion is about that private heap: how many bytes of spans it mapped for
a burst, how many it gave back once the burst became garbage, and whether it
still serves the same size class afterwards. Objects it allocates are the
private collector's and invisible to the ambient one, and the collections that
matter are the explicit `gcCollectRoots` calls this program makes itself.

CORRECTION: "None of them depends on whether the ambient (libbitrt) collector
ever runs" was true in spirit but understated — it does not literally never
run. Measured, aarch64-macos, 5 runs each policy: default `collections=0`
every time; `BIT_GC=stress` 48266, 48257, 48265, 48265, 48295 (denominator:
5/5, spread ~0.08%), well above stress.bit's documented ~101 scheduler-loop
floor. Confirmed default and `BIT_GC=stress` stdout are both byte-identical
to `.expected`.

Unlike ../chanmakeroots/../chanroots/../gcindexchurn/../gcreserve (#4184),
this program's own final assertion closes the rooting question directly:
`arena` is read again at the very end ("reached here so it stays rooted
across every ambient collection") and the result is printed as `rooted`, so
the arena's live interval genuinely extends through the last ambient
collection this program could see, and the printed value proves it did.
